course: dictionary
lesson: 3944
title: Fully: completely, or at least
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word fully is built from full, and it usually means completely, in every way. When you meet an unknown word, read the words around it, because they are its context. «The phone was fully charged before the trip.» A charged phone has its power all the way up, so fully must mean completely.
explain: Here fully means in every way: «Toad apologises fully and promises to do nothing without the Rat's advice.» Toad does not say sorry a little. He says sorry all the way, with nothing held back. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Try completely in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Fully can also mean at least, or as much as. «Fully half the class was ill.» The clue is half: half the class is a big number, so fully means at least half. Swap in at least and see if the sentence still works.
fact: Fully means completely, or in every way.
fact: «Fully grown» means grown all the way, with nothing left to grow.
fact: «Fully half the class was ill» means at least half the class was ill.
fact: In safety rules, «fully awake» means awake all the way, not just half awake.
fact: Fully often comes before a word such as charged, booked, dressed, or understand.
exercise: «The phone was fully charged before the trip.» What does fully mean here? | *Completely | Loudly | Partly, a little | Slowly
exercise: «Fully half the class was ill.» What does fully mean here? | *At least, as much as | Quietly | Completely, in every way | Happily
exercise: «Around midsummer the sky over Britain never gets fully dark.» What does fully dark mean? | Dark only at night | Bright and sunny | Partly dark, with a few stars | *Completely dark
test: «The children were fully dressed for the party.» What does fully mean? | Only a little | Slowly | Not at all | *Completely
test: Ella says, «The hotel was fully booked.» What does fully mean? | Hungry | Half-finished | Very cheap | *Completely, with no space left
exam: «Fully half of the crowd cheered.» What does fully mean here? | Quietly | Slowly | Completely, in every way | *At least, as much as
